package settings

import (
	"net/url"
	"time"
)

// LegacySettings configures the legacy Bitcoin wire protocol service.
type LegacySettings struct {
	WorkingDir                       string        `key:"legacy_workingDir" desc:"Working directory for legacy operations" default:"../../data" category:"Legacy" usage:"Path for legacy data storage" type:"string" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSpecifies the directory where the legacy Bitcoin protocol service stores its operational data.\n\n### How It Works\nThis directory contains:\n- **Peer database** - Known legacy node addresses\n- **Orphan transactions** - Transactions waiting for parent inputs\n- **Temporary files** - Protocol processing artifacts\n\n### Options\n- **Relative path** - Resolved relative to binary location\n- **Absolute path** - Full filesystem path\n\n### Recommendations\n- Use absolute paths in production for predictability\n- Ensure sufficient disk space for orphan transaction storage\n- Place on fast storage for better performance"`
	ListenAddresses                  []string      `key:"legacy_listen_addresses" desc:"Listen addresses for legacy Bitcoin protocol (pipe-separated)" default:"" category:"Legacy" usage:"For backward compatibility with older nodes" type:"[]string" longdesc:"### Purpose\nConfigures network addresses where the node accepts incoming connections from legacy Bitcoin protocol clients.\n\n### How It Works\nEnables backward compatibility with:\n- BSV Blockchain nodes not yet upgraded to Teranode\n- Legacy mining software\n- Traditional Bitcoin protocol tools\n\n### Options\n- **ip:port format** - Multiple addresses separated by pipes\n- **Empty string** - Disables legacy protocol listening entirely\n\n### Examples\n| Value | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| 0.0.0.0:8333 | Listen on all interfaces |\n| 192.168.1.10:8333 | Listen on specific IP |\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave empty unless you need to connect with legacy nodes\n- Use firewall rules to restrict access in production"`
	ConnectPeers                     []string      `key:"legacy_connect_peers" desc:"Legacy peers to connect to (pipe-separated)" default:"" category:"Legacy" usage:"For syncing with legacy Bitcoin nodes" type:"[]string" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSpecifies legacy Bitcoin node addresses that this node should actively connect to as outbound peers.\n\n### How It Works\nThe node initiates connections to these addresses on startup and maintains persistent connections for:\n- Blockchain synchronization with legacy nodes\n- Transaction relay to legacy infrastructure\n- Block announcement propagation\n\n### Options\n- **ip:port format** - Multiple addresses separated by pipes\n- **Empty string** - No outbound legacy connections\n\n### Use Cases\n- Syncing with existing BSV Blockchain infrastructure\n- Connecting to known trusted legacy nodes\n- Private network setups with legacy components\n\n### Recommendations\n- Only connect to trusted legacy nodes\n- Monitor connection health in logs"`
	OrphanEvictionDuration           time.Duration `key:"legacy_orphanEvictionDuration" desc:"Duration before evicting orphan transactions" default:"10m" category:"Legacy" usage:"How long to keep orphan transactions" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls how long orphan transactions are retained before being automatically removed from memory.\n\n### How It Works\nOrphan transactions are transactions received where one or more parent inputs are unknown. The node holds these temporarily hoping the parent transactions arrive soon.\n\nAfter this duration, orphans are evicted to:\n- Prevent memory exhaustion\n- Clean up transactions that will likely never complete\n- Protect against orphan flooding attacks\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Longer duration** - More time for parent transactions to arrive, but uses more memory\n- **Shorter duration** - Less memory usage, but may prematurely evict valid orphans\n\n### Recommendations\n- Default of 10 minutes works well for most networks\n- Reduce on memory-constrained systems\n- Increase if experiencing frequent orphan re-requests"`
	MaxOrphanTxs                     int           `key:"legacy_maxOrphanTxs" desc:"Maximum number of orphan transactions held in memory" default:"100" category:"Legacy" usage:"Hard cap on the orphan transaction pool" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nBounds the size of the orphan transaction pool to prevent memory-exhaustion DoS from a peer flooding unique orphan transactions faster than the TTL eviction rate.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen the orphan pool reaches this cap, inserting a new orphan evicts the oldest entry first (by insertion time). The evicted orphan is given one final validation attempt, mirroring TTL-based eviction semantics.\n\n### Recommendations\n- Default of 100 matches Bitcoin Core's historical orphan pool size.\n- Raise only if you have reason to expect legitimate bursts of orphans larger than the default and have memory headroom.\n- Setting to 0 disables the cap (legacy unbounded behaviour) and is not recommended on public networks."`
	PrintInvMessages                 bool          `key:"legacy_printInvMessages" desc:"Print inventory messages for debugging" default:"false" category:"Legacy" usage:"Enable for legacy protocol debugging" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nEnables verbose logging of inventory (inv) messages received through the legacy Bitcoin protocol.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen enabled, every inventory message is logged including:\n- Transaction announcements from peers\n- Block announcements\n- Other inventory vectors\n\n### Options\n- **true** - Log all inventory messages\n- **false** - Suppress inventory message logging\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Enabled** - Useful for debugging protocol issues, generates high log volume\n- **Disabled** - Cleaner logs, less visibility into protocol traffic\n\n### Recommendations\n- **Disable in production** - Very verbose output\n- Enable temporarily when troubleshooting legacy peer connectivity\n- Use in conjunction with log rotation when enabled"`
	GRPCAddress                      string        `key:"legacy_grpcAddress" desc:"gRPC address for Legacy service" default:"" category:"Legacy" usage:"Address for legacy service communication" type:"string" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSpecifies the gRPC address used by other Teranode services to communicate with the legacy service.\n\n### How It Works\nOther microservices connect to this address to:\n- Request data from legacy peers\n- Coordinate transaction propagation\n- Exchange blockchain state information\n\n### Options\n- **host:port format** - Full address for the gRPC endpoint\n- **Empty string** - Legacy service gRPC client disabled\n\n### Recommendations\n- Configure when running distributed multi-service deployments\n- Use internal network addresses for security\n- Ensure firewall allows traffic between services"`
	AllowBlockPriority               bool          `key:"legacy_allowBlockPriority" desc:"Enable the BSV multistream BlockPriority stream policy for legacy P2P" default:"true" category:"Legacy" usage:"Negotiate multiple TCP streams per peer, separating block traffic from general traffic" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nEnables the BSV multistream protocol (BlockPriority stream policy) for legacy P2P connections. Peers negotiate multiple TCP streams per association, separating bulk block/header transfer (DATA1) from latency-sensitive control traffic (GENERAL).\n\n### How It Works\n- Advertised in the protoconf handshake; peers that also support BlockPriority open a DATA1 stream\n- Block and header replies are delivered on DATA1, isolating multi-GB transfers from control messages\n- Required for reliable download of large (multi-GB) blocks from BSV mainnet sync peers\n\n### Options\n- **true** - Negotiate BlockPriority multistream with capable peers (default)\n- **false** - Single-stream legacy protocol only\n\n### Recommendations\n- Keep enabled for mainnet/testnet sync, where large blocks require the dedicated block stream"`
	GRPCListenAddress                string        `key:"legacy_grpcListenAddress" desc:"gRPC listen address for Legacy service" default:"" category:"Legacy" usage:"Port for incoming legacy gRPC connections" type:"string" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSpecifies the address where the legacy service listens for incoming gRPC connections from other Teranode services.\n\n### How It Works\nThe legacy service exposes its internal API on this address, allowing other microservices to:\n- Query legacy peer state\n- Submit transactions for legacy propagation\n- Access legacy-specific functionality\n\n### Options\n- **host:port format** - Address to bind the gRPC server\n- **Empty string** - Legacy gRPC server disabled\n\n### Recommendations\n- Bind to internal network interfaces only\n- Use **0.0.0.0:port** for container deployments\n- Pair with **GRPCAddress** for bidirectional communication"`
	SavePeers                        bool          `key:"legacy_savePeers" desc:"Persist legacy peer list to disk" default:"false" category:"Legacy" usage:"Enable for peer persistence across restarts" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls whether discovered legacy peer addresses are saved to disk for use across node restarts.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen enabled:\n- Peer addresses discovered during operation are written to disk\n- On restart, saved peers are loaded immediately\n- Reduces time needed to establish network connectivity\n\nPeer data is stored in the **WorkingDir** location.\n\n### Options\n- **true** - Persist peers to disk\n- **false** - Peers discovered fresh each startup\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Enabled** - Faster startup, requires disk write permissions\n- **Disabled** - Clean slate each start, no stale peer data\n\n### Recommendations\n- Enable for production nodes with stable peer networks\n- Disable for ephemeral/test deployments\n- Periodically clean peer database to remove stale entries"`
	AllowSyncCandidateFromLocalPeers bool          `key:"legacy_allowSyncCandidateFromLocalPeers" desc:"Allow sync candidate from local peers" default:"false" category:"Legacy" usage:"Enable for local network sync" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls whether peers on local/private networks are considered as blockchain sync candidates.\n\n### How It Works\nBy default, the node excludes local network peers (RFC1918 addresses) from sync candidate selection as a security measure. When enabled:\n- Local network peers can be chosen for initial block download\n- Private testnet setups work correctly\n- Development environments can sync without public peers\n\n### Options\n- **true** - Local network peers eligible for sync\n- **false** - Only public network peers eligible for sync\n\n### Use Cases\n- Private/isolated blockchain networks\n- Development and testing environments\n- Air-gapped deployments\n\n### Recommendations\n- **Keep disabled for mainnet** - Prevents local network attacks\n- Enable only for private networks and testing\n- Combine with **ConnectPeers** for controlled sync sources"`
	TempStore                        *url.URL      `key:"temp_store" desc:"Temporary storage URL for legacy operations" default:"file://./data/tempstore" category:"Legacy" usage:"Temporary data storage location" type:"url" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSpecifies the storage location for temporary data generated during legacy protocol operations.\n\n### How It Works\nTemporary storage is used for:\n- Intermediate block processing data\n- Transaction validation scratch space\n- Protocol message buffering\n\n### Options\n- **file://path** - Local filesystem storage\n- Other URL schemes supported by the blob store\n\n### Examples\n| Value | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| file://./data/tempstore | Relative path (default) |\n| file:///var/teranode/temp | Absolute path |\n\n### Recommendations\n- Use fast storage (SSD) for better performance\n- Ensure sufficient space for block processing\n- Configure automatic cleanup or use tmpfs for ephemeral data"`
	PeerIdleTimeout                  time.Duration `key:"legacy_peerIdleTimeout" desc:"Timeout for idle legacy peers" default:"125s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Disconnect after this idle time (ping interval is 2 mins)" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDefines the maximum time a legacy peer connection can remain idle before being disconnected.\n\n### How It Works\nThe Bitcoin protocol uses a 2-minute ping/pong mechanism to detect dead connections. This timeout:\n- Starts counting from last message received\n- Resets on any incoming protocol message\n- Triggers disconnection when exceeded\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Higher timeout** - More tolerant of network issues, slower detection of dead peers\n- **Lower timeout** - Faster cleanup of dead connections, may disconnect slow peers prematurely\n\n### Recommendations\n- Default of 125 seconds provides buffer above 2-minute ping interval\n- Account for network jitter and latency\n- Increase for high-latency network conditions\n- Do not set below 120 seconds (ping interval)"`
	MaxAddnodePeers                  int           `key:"legacy_maxAddnodePeers" desc:"Maximum peers connected via addnode, budgeted separately from MaxPeers" default:"8" category:"Legacy" usage:"Ceiling on named (addnode) peers, independent of the automatic outbound target" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nNamed peers are budgeted separately from the automatic outbound tier, so configuring them never costs the node one of its independently chosen outbound addresses. That separation needs its own ceiling, or an addnode list could grow the node without limit.\n\n### How It Works\n- Bounds the addnode list only; connect-only peers (**legacy_connect_peers**) are the node's entire connectivity and are never capped\n- Additive to **MaxPeers**, which bounds the automatic outbound and inbound tiers, matching svnode where the addnode semaphore is sized independently of maxconnections\n- Enforced both on the startup list and on the runtime addnode RPC\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **8**, matching svnode's -maxaddnodeconnections default"`
	MaxFeelerPeers                   int           `key:"legacy_maxFeelerPeers" desc:"Peer slots reserved for short-lived feeler probes; 0 disables feelers entirely" default:"1" category:"Legacy" usage:"Budget for probes that verify addresses the node is not otherwise dialling" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nThe node only ever learns that an address works as a side effect of a connection it wanted anyway, so the pool of addresses known to be reachable decays. A feeler is a short-lived probe: it connects to an address the node is not using, completes the version exchange to prove somebody is home, and hangs up. The address is then marked as verified, so when a peer is later lost the replacement is drawn from addresses that answered recently.\n\n### How It Works\n- The reservation is taken out of the peer-admission ceiling, not out of the automatic outbound target, so probing never costs the node a real outbound peer. svnode does the same arithmetic, subtracting its feeler budget from inbound capacity (net.cpp:1261)\n- The value is also the hard cap on probes running at once\n- Probes only start once the automatic outbound tier is already at its target\n\n### Options\n- **1** (default) — matches svnode, which runs a single feeler\n- **0** — disables the feature completely: no probes, and no slot reserved\n\n### When the node reserves nothing regardless\nTwo further conditions switch feelers off on their own, and both log the reason at startup:\n- **Connect-only mode** (**legacy_connect_peers** set) — the node's whole connectivity is the configured list and it stops discovering peers for itself, so a verified address has nothing to feed\n- **A cap too tight to hold the reservation** — if reserving would push the peer-admission ceiling below the automatic outbound target, the probe is given up rather than the tier, because a node in that state would sit permanently below target, dialling and being refused by its own door. Note the shipped cap is **legacy_config_MaxPeers = 20** against a target of 8, so there is ample room; lowering that cap towards the target is what brings this into play\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **1**. The reservation costs one inbound slot out of the peer cap, and buys an address book whose entries have been checked."`
	FeelerInterval                   time.Duration `key:"legacy_feelerInterval" desc:"Average gap between feeler probes" default:"120s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Mean of the randomised delay between probes" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nPaces the feeler probe. Matches svnode's FEELER_INTERVAL of 120 seconds (net.h:88).\n\n### How It Works\nThe gap between probes is randomised around this mean rather than fixed. A fixed period would be a fingerprint: an observer seeing probes exactly two minutes apart can recognise the node across address changes and predict the next one. A randomised gap leaks neither. svnode randomises the same way, in PoissonNextSend.\n\n### Options\n- **120s** (default) — svnode's cadence, about thirty probes an hour\n- **Shorter** — verifies the address book faster, useful when testing\n- **Below 1s** — accepted but not honoured: the probe loop only checks its deadline once a second, so the realised gap never falls below a second and the realised mean of a sub-second setting is higher than the one configured\n- **0 or negative** — falls back to 120s with a warning. This is deliberately NOT a disable lever; use **legacy_maxFeelerPeers = 0** for that, so a node cannot end up with feelers switched off while still paying for the reserved slot\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **120s** outside testing."`
	FeelerHandshakeTimeout           time.Duration `key:"legacy_feelerHandshakeTimeout" desc:"How long a feeler probe waits for a version message" default:"25s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Must be shorter than the 30s peer negotiate timeout so a mute host is hung up by the feeler, not logged as a lost peer" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nBounds how long a feeler waits for the far side to identify itself before hanging up.\n\n### How It Works\nAssociateConnection starts the peer package's own 30s negotiate timer within microseconds of the probe starting this one, so an equal or longer feeler deadline cannot be relied on to fire first. Whichever fires first owns the teardown. If the peer package wins, on a host that accepts TCP and then stays silent, the disconnect is logged at warning and counted as a lost peer; the feeler hangs up quietly instead. The deadline therefore has to leave the peer timer a clear margin.\n\n### Options\n- **25s** (default) — five seconds inside the peer timeout\n- **Shorter** — useful when testing\n- **0 or negative** — falls back to 25s with a warning\n- **30s or more** — reduced to 29s with a warning; at or above the peer timeout the peer package hangs the connection up first, at warning level\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **25s** outside testing."`
	ReplenishInterval                time.Duration `key:"legacy_replenishInterval" desc:"How often the connection manager tops outbound peers back up to the target" default:"2s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Connection supply cadence; faster replenish closes the gap left by a disconnect" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nEvery disconnect leaves the node below its outbound target until the connection manager notices and dials a replacement. A slow replenish cadence turns each disconnect into a measurable stretch of reduced download capacity — visible during IBD as the 'quiet' half of the busy/quiet oscillation.\n\n### How It Works\n- On each tick the connection manager compares live outbound peers against the target and dials the shortfall\n- Faster ticks mean the shortfall is closed in seconds rather than tens of seconds\n- A freed outbound slot also wakes the loop directly, so a lost peer is usually replaced within about a second\n\n### Options\n- **2s** (default) — connection supply tracks demand closely\n- **0** — rollback lever: restores the historical fixed one-minute cadence and disables the event-driven wake\n\n### Recommendations\n- Leave at **2s**; the cost of a faster tick is a cheap periodic comparison, the benefit is that connection supply tracks demand closely"`
	PeerProcessingTimeout            time.Duration `key:"legacy_peerProcessingTimeout" desc:"Timeout for processing legacy peer messages" default:"3m" category:"Legacy" usage:"Maximum time for processing block messages" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDefines the maximum time allowed to process a single legacy protocol message before timing out.\n\n### How It Works\nSome protocol messages, particularly large blocks, can take significant time to process. This timeout:\n- Applies per-message during processing\n- Prevents indefinite hangs on malformed data\n- Protects against slow-loris style attacks\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Higher timeout** - Accommodates larger blocks, slower detection of stuck processing\n- **Lower timeout** - Faster failure detection, may timeout on legitimate large blocks\n\n### Recommendations\n- Default of 3 minutes handles most block sizes\n- Increase for networks with very large blocks\n- Monitor for timeout errors in logs to adjust\n- Consider hardware performance when tuning"`

	BlockDownloadTimeoutBasePercent    int64 `key:"legacy_blockDownloadTimeoutBasePercent" desc:"Wall-clock ceiling on one block download at the chain tip, as a percentage of the target block interval; floored at 30 minutes, so values at or below 300 have no effect on a 10-minute chain" default:"100" category:"Legacy" usage:"100 means one block interval" type:"int64" longdesc:"### Purpose\nCaps how long a single block transfer may be kept alive by throughput-based deadline extension. Expressed as a percentage of the target block interval rather than an absolute duration, so the same value is correct on any chain regardless of its block time. Mirrors svnode's -blockdownloadtimeoutbasepercent.\n\n### How It Works\nceiling = max(blockInterval * (base + perPeer * otherPeersDownloading) / 100, 30 minutes). At the tip the base is this value; while catching up it is BlockDownloadTimeoutBaseIBDPercent instead. The 30-minute floor is peer.MaxBlockDownloadTime, the flat ceiling this calculation replaced: the scaling is there to widen the deadline for multi-peer downloads, so it is not allowed to narrow it.\n\n### Recommendations\n- **100** (default) matches svnode. On mainnet that is 10 minutes, which is under the 30-minute floor, so the effective tip ceiling is 30 minutes. Raise this above 300 to widen the tip; values below that make no difference."`
	BlockDownloadTimeoutBaseIBDPercent int64 `key:"legacy_blockDownloadTimeoutBaseIBDPercent" desc:"Wall-clock ceiling on one block download while catching up, as a percentage of the target block interval; floored at 30 minutes, which the 600 default clears on a 10-minute chain" default:"600" category:"Legacy" usage:"600 means six block intervals" type:"int64" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDuring initial block download a historical block is large and our own validation backpressure delays the read loop, so the tip-sized ceiling aborts honest transfers just short of completion and forces a full re-download from another peer — the most expensive failure mode in a sync, because it wastes the bandwidth already spent.\n\n### How It Works\nReplaces BlockDownloadTimeoutBasePercent in the ceiling calculation whenever the sync manager reports that we are catching up. At the tip the original value applies unchanged, so a dead tip peer is still reaped promptly. Like the tip ceiling this is floored at 30 minutes, which at the default never binds.\n\n### Recommendations\n- **600** (default) matches svnode. On mainnet that is 60 minutes.\n- Set equal to BlockDownloadTimeoutBasePercent to restore a single ceiling everywhere."`
	BlockDownloadTimeoutPerPeerPercent int64 `key:"legacy_blockDownloadTimeoutPerPeerPercent" desc:"Extra ceiling allowed per other peer we are downloading blocks from, as a percentage of the target block interval; the total is floored at 30 minutes, so this can only add patience" default:"50" category:"Legacy" usage:"Compensates for our own downstream link being shared" type:"int64" longdesc:"### Purpose\nWhen blocks are being pulled from several peers at once each transfer is legitimately slower, because our own downstream link is shared between them. Without compensation, fetching in parallel makes every peer look stalled and gets them all disconnected. Mirrors svnode's -blockdownloadtimeoutperpeerpercent.\n\n### How It Works\nAdds this percentage to the ceiling for each OTHER peer with a block download in flight. Only peers with a genuine outstanding request are counted, so a peer cannot inflate our patience by advertising blocks it does not have. The total is floored at 30 minutes, so this setting can only add patience.\n\n### Recommendations\n- **50** (default) matches svnode.\n- **0** disables the compensation and leaves the base percentage and the 30-minute floor to decide the ceiling."`

	MultiPeerBlockDownload bool `key:"legacy_multiPeerBlockDownload" desc:"Download block bodies from every eligible peer at once during legacy sync, instead of only from the sync peer" default:"true" category:"Legacy" usage:"false restores the single-sync-peer download path" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDuring headers-first sync the node knows each block hash it needs up to the next checkpoint. With this on, those blocks are spread across every eligible peer, which is how SV Node downloads (it has no sync peer for block bodies). With it off, every block is asked of the sync peer alone.\n\n### How It Works\nA peer is eligible if it is a connected sync candidate. Below the last checkpoint each block, in height order, goes to the measured peer with the lowest (blocks owed + 1) / rate, the peer with more room on a tie (simple_schedule.go). A block size is not known before the download, so the schedule uses none: a fast peer gets more blocks and the nearer ones, and no block waits while a peer has room. A peer with no measured rate gets one block from the top of the download window; with no rates at all, each peer takes one block in height sequence. Measured rates are kept by address in legacy-peer-rates.json in legacy_workingDir across a restart. A peer whose advertised best height reaches the block is preferred; when no peer with room advertises it, one is asked anyway, because an advertised height is a lower bound that goes stale. A measured peer holds up to legacy_maxBlocksInTransitPerPeer blocks scaled by its rate against the fastest peer's, a peer with no rate 1, and legacy_blockDownloadWindow caps the total across the node.\n\nA block is owed by one peer at a time, with three exceptions:\n- a block whose owners have sent nothing for the 60-second retry window (blockRequestRetryInterval) is asked of another peer on the next download pass: above the last checkpoint any block, one block per quiet owner; below it only a block with two or more active owners, since a block with one active owner stays with it and the watcher judges it;\n- during headers-first sync, a block above the committed tip that has been arriving for 30 seconds at under 100 KB/s, and will not finish before the chain needs it, is asked of one other peer and the slow peer is disconnected (the frontier race, frontier_race.go). Every copy of the block must be that slow, a further copy waits 30 seconds after the last one, and at most three peers send or are expected to send a block at once; a peer let off a block that sends nothing is not counted. The slow peer is kept connected when the peer asked is one that was let off the block and asked again, or, at the three-copy limit, when such a peer is still connected and sends nothing.\n- during headers-first sync, the watcher (watcher.go) examines up to 64 owed blocks from the tip every 5 seconds. A block that will land 30 seconds or more after each block below it is also asked of one other peer: for a block arriving, whose size its first bytes declare, the peer that lands a full copy in half the owner's time; for a queued block, the fastest peer with twice the owner's rate that lands the block, at the recent mean size, in half the owner's time. A copy with more than half its bytes gets no extra request unless it will take more than 5 minutes, and a block whose every copy arrives under 100 KB/s is left to the race, with no block above it counted as late. A block gets one such extra request.\n\n### Options\n- **true (default)** - blocks come from every eligible peer. A sync peer that stalls is demoted rather than disconnected: it keeps its connection and the blocks it owes. A notfound releases the named blocks so another peer can be asked.\n- **false** - every block is asked of the sync peer, its queue sized by the block-size ladder. A stalled sync peer is disconnected and replaced, and a notfound is only logged.\n\n### Note\nWith this on, blocks routinely arrive before their parent. They are held on disk in the block park until the parent commits. The park is always on and needs a file:// temp_store."`

	MaxBlocksInTransitPerPeer int `key:"legacy_maxBlocksInTransitPerPeer" desc:"Maximum block bodies one peer may be downloading at once during legacy sync" default:"16" category:"Legacy" usage:"svnode uses a hardcoded 16; not reduced for large blocks, the park backstop guards the disk instead" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nCaps how deep one peer's block queue may get, which is what stops a single peer being handed every wanted block while other peers sit idle. svnode's equivalent, MAX_BLOCKS_IN_TRANSIT_PER_PEER, is a hardcoded 16.\n\n### How It Works\nWith legacy_multiPeerBlockDownload on (the default) this is the most outstanding blocks a measured peer holds; a peer whose speed is not yet known holds 1. A measured peer holds this value scaled by its rate against the fastest peer's, rounded up, at least 1: a peer at a tenth of the fastest rate holds 2 of 16 (peerQueueDepth in block_scheduler.go). The block-size ladder does not apply and the fan-out is never narrowed for large blocks: block bodies stream to disk, so there is no memory reason to.\n\nWhat protects the disk is a separate backstop, parkBackstopBytes in block_scheduler.go: once the bytes held ahead of the chain, parked plus arriving, reach 100 GiB, no block further ahead is asked for until the chain catches up. SV Node has no byte limit; its brake is the block download window, 1,024 by default (-blockdownloadwindow).\n\nWith legacy_multiPeerBlockDownload off the per-peer depth is not used: only the sync peer is asked, and the block-size ladder sizes its queue from the average recent block size (20 below 100 MiB, 10 below 200 MiB, 5 below 500 MiB, 3 below 1 GiB, 2 below 2 GiB, 1 at 2 GiB or more).\n\n### Read In Every Mode\n- The streaming pipeline's download-admission budget is this value times four (pipelineBlockSlotPeerAllowance).\n- The block-download timeout budget bounds how many peers can hold a download at once as legacy_blockDownloadWindow divided by this value, rounded up (maxPeersWithBlockDownloads in the peer package).\n\n### Values\n- **16 (default)** - svnode's depth\n- **lower** - shallower queues, more even spread across peers, more round trips\n- **higher** - deeper queues per peer, and a proportionally larger admission budget"`

	BlockDownloadWindow int `key:"legacy_blockDownloadWindow" desc:"Maximum block bodies the whole node may be downloading at once during legacy sync" default:"1024" category:"Legacy" usage:"svnode uses DEFAULT_BLOCK_DOWNLOAD_WINDOW of 1024; counts blocks owed by every peer together" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nThe node-wide ceiling on outstanding block requests, and the same figure wantedBlocks uses as its read-ahead depth: how far above the committed tip a block may be asked for at all. Where legacy_maxBlocksInTransitPerPeer bounds one peer, this bounds the sum over every peer, so adding peers cannot grow the queue without limit, and it is also what decides how much disk the park can hold, since a block fetched far ahead of the one being waited on cannot be committed when it arrives and sits parked until everything between it and the chain has landed.\n\nsvnode's DEFAULT_BLOCK_DOWNLOAD_WINDOW is also 1024, but it is not the same quantity and the matching number is a coincidence worth knowing about. svnode's window is a HEIGHT RANGE, per peer, measured from the last block that peer and this node agree on; this one is a COUNT of blocks outstanding across the whole node, doing both jobs at once.\n\n### How It Works\nBefore each pass the node counts the blocks it is already owed and only hands out the difference, and wantedBlocksFromCache does not name a height more than this many above the committed tip in the first place.\n\n### Values\n- **1024 (default)** - svnode's window\n- **lower** - a hard ceiling on how many blocks may be outstanding across all peers, and how far ahead of the tip they may be\n\n### Note\nThe node-wide ceiling on outstanding requests is applied only when legacy_multiPeerBlockDownload is on; the read-ahead depth applies on the common fetchHeaderBlocks path either way."`

	ParkStoreTimeout         time.Duration `key:"legacy_parkStoreTimeout" desc:"Deadline carried by each park blob store operation, bounding the wait for the file store's shared permits on the commit goroutine and the peer read loop" default:"10s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Bounds how long one park store call can hold up block download or the in-order commit" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nThe park's blob store calls share the file store's process-wide permits (256 write, 768 read) with subtree writes, transaction writes and both persisters, and the store waits up to 25 seconds for a permit. Several of those calls sit on paths that hold up sync while they wait: the in-order commit goroutine, and a peer's read loop. A caller deadline is the only thing that shortens the wait.\n\n### What It Bounds\nEach of these carries this deadline:\n- writing a block's converted record, on the peer's read loop at the end of the stream; on expiry the write is treated as this node's own storage fault, the peer is kept and the block is asked for again;\n- reading a parked record back to commit it, together with the check that the subtree files it names are all present (one deadline over the pair); on expiry the block stays parked and the sweep retries it;\n- the held-block probe the inv path and the download pass make, which is the same read and check; on expiry the block is treated as not held and downloaded again, which costs bandwidth but does not stop the chain;\n- deleting a record after commit; on expiry the entry is forgotten and the file is left for the next start;\n- each blockchain lookup the park sweep makes on the commit goroutine.\n\nThe restart scan does not use a deadline per file. It has one budget of three times this value for the whole scan, and files it does not reach are left for the next start.\n\n### What It Does NOT Bound\nThe work the store does once it holds a permit. The open, copy and unlink after the permit do not consult a context, so on a hung mount this setting buys nothing.\n\n### Values\n- **10s (default)** - rides out a normal burst of permit contention and bounds the worst case at one stalled call per block.\n- Values below one second are raised to one second. The park itself has no off switch; it needs a file:// temp_store."`
	Upnp                     bool          `key:"legacy_upnp" desc:"Enable UPnP for automatic port mapping" default:"false" category:"Legacy" usage:"Enable automatic port forwarding via UPnP" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nEnables Universal Plug and Play (UPnP) for automatic port forwarding on compatible routers.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen enabled, the node will:\n- Attempt to discover UPnP-capable routers on the local network\n- Automatically configure port forwarding for incoming connections\n- Periodically renew the port mapping lease\n\n### Options\n- **true** - Enable UPnP port mapping\n- **false** - Disable UPnP (manual port forwarding required)\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Enabled** - Easier setup, requires UPnP support on router\n- **Disabled** - More secure, requires manual router configuration\n\n### Recommendations\n- Keep disabled in production for security\n- Enable for home/development setups behind NAT\n- Ensure router supports UPnP if enabled"`
	PeerRegistryEnabled      bool          `key:"legacy_peerRegistryEnabled" desc:"Mirror connected legacy peers into the centralized peer registry" default:"true" category:"Legacy" usage:"Makes legacy peers visible in the dashboard alongside libp2p peers" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nMirrors every connected legacy (Bitcoin wire protocol) peer into the centralized peer registry hosted by the blockchain service, so the dashboard can show legacy peers alongside libp2p peers.\n\n### How It Works\nA reconcile goroutine in the legacy service periodically snapshots its connected peers and registers each one with transport type TRANSPORT_WIRE_PROTOCOL. Entries carry wire-protocol-only fields: direction, protocol version, service flags, ping, clock offset, starting height, sync-peer flag and wire connect time.\n\nThis is a read-only visibility path. It feeds no sync, catchup or peer-selection decision.\n\n### Options\n- **true** - Register legacy peers (default)\n- **false** - Kill switch; the reconcile loop does not start\n\n### Recommendations\n- Keep enabled for operator visibility\n- Disable only if the extra registry traffic is unwelcome on a node with very many legacy connections"`
	PeerRegistrySyncInterval time.Duration `key:"legacy_peerRegistrySyncInterval" desc:"How often legacy peers are reconciled into the centralized peer registry" default:"10s" category:"Legacy" usage:"Lower values refresh the dashboard sooner and cost more registry traffic" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls how often the legacy service pushes its connected-peer snapshot into the centralized peer registry.\n\n### How It Works\nEach tick snapshots the connected legacy peers and pushes only what changed. An unchanged peer costs no RPC. A peer that disappeared is marked disconnected once, then left for the registry TTL to reap.\n\n### Trade-offs\n- **Shorter interval** - A connect or disconnect appears sooner in the dashboard, more registry RPCs\n- **Longer interval** - Less traffic, staler dashboard\n\n### Recommendations\n- Default of 10 seconds is well below the legacy 2-minute ping interval\n- Values under 1 second are pointless: the underlying peer statistics do not change that fast"`
}
